<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/" type="topic" id="canvas-editing">
  <info><link type="guide" xref="canvas"/><desc>Draw, Erase, and Crop in coyote.</desc></info>
  <title>Draw, Erase, and Crop</title>
  <p>Choose <gui>File</gui> → <gui>New</gui> for a blank drawing, <gui>Import...</gui> for PNG, JPEG, or static SVG, or <gui>Edit</gui> → <gui>Paste</gui> for a PNG or JPEG offered by the clipboard. Select the <gui>Pencil</gui>, <gui>Eraser</gui>, or <gui>Crop</gui> icon below the menu bar, or use the matching <gui>Edit</gui> menu item. The active icon is highlighted. Hover over an icon to see its name. <gui>Pencil</gui> is selected by default.</p>
  <p>PNG and JPEG imported into Canvas are kept at their original dimensions—Canvas never automatically resizes an import. Regular files selected with <gui>Import...</gui> are streamed and have no compressed file-size cutoff. In a saved session, larger PNGs and supported single-scan JPEGs use disk tiles, up to 32,768 pixels per side and 2 GiB of tiled data per batch. Some JPEG encodings, including progressive and CMYK, must fit the ordinary limit of 8,192 pixels per side and 33,554,432 pixels per image. Memory-only sessions, clipboard and agent imports also retain the ordinary limits. SVG has separate input and rendering limits. If an original is too large to attach, crop a revision to the region you want to share.</p>
  <p>On a tiled original, Eraser shows its guide while you draw and applies the erase when you release.</p>
  <p>Selecting a single saved revision or choosing <gui>Show in Canvas</gui> displays that exact saved image; it does not replace or discard a working draft. When the displayed saved revision is writable and <gui>View</gui> → <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on, <gui>Pencil</gui>, <gui>Eraser</gui>, and <gui>Crop</gui> are available. There is no separate Edit Revision command. A completed pointer or finger stroke, or <gui>Apply Crop</gui>, begins a working copy based on the displayed revision. If the previous working copy has unsaved changes, Canvas preserves them in a new revision before switching. Starting or canceling a gesture without completing it does not change drafts.</p>
  <p>Use the session-local <gui>Pencil size</gui> and <gui>Eraser size</gui> controls to choose source-pixel dimensions from 1 to 64. Each session starts with Pencil size 3 and Eraser size 16. The active size is captured when a gesture begins and stays fixed through that stroke. Brush size is independent of zoom.</p>
  <p>Use the <gui>Pencil color</gui> swatch to choose opaque RGB for future Pencil marks. For mouse strokes and finger strokes, and for pen strokes when pressure is unavailable, Pencil marks are opaque. When pen pressure is available, it changes only the alpha of deposited Pencil marks, not their size; the mark color and size remain those captured for the gesture. Marks are placed at distance-based intervals along the path. Retracing within one gesture deposits again and can darken the ink. Changing the swatch does not recolor existing ink or switch tools. Saved revisions and attachments retain the marks as drawn.</p>
  <p>Draw with the pen tip or mouse. Select <gui>Eraser</gui> in the toolbar or <gui>Edit</gui> menu to remove ink, without changing the background. Erasing ignores pressure. A recognized tablet eraser tip uses the captured Eraser size without changing the selected tool; selected <gui>Crop</gui> takes precedence. Ink disappears in the preview as you drag. The preview removes ink only: saved revisions and attachments are unchanged until release commits the whole gesture as one <gui>Undo</gui>/<gui>Redo</gui> edit. Press <key>Escape</key> to cancel and restore the ink without an edit or revision change. If live preview is unavailable, Canvas shows the unchanged drawing and brush guide. Brush and guide stay at the captured size regardless of zoom.</p>
  <p>For touchscreen editing, enable <guiseq><gui>Options</gui><gui>Edit with Finger</gui></guiseq>. It is off at the start of each session. With <gui>Pencil</gui> or <gui>Eraser</gui> selected, drag one finger on a writable drawing while <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on. The operation and size are fixed when the contact starts: Pencil paints opaque marks at the captured Pencil size; Eraser removes ink only at the captured Eraser size and leaves the background unchanged. Ink removal is visible while you drag; lift commits one undoable edit, while Escape cancels and restores ink without changing the revision. Sizes stay constant at different zoom levels. Extra contacts do not draw or take over. Touch panning remains available when the option is off or touch editing is otherwise ineligible, including read-only drawings, hidden markup, and <gui>Crop</gui> or Zoom modes. The setting is session-local and does not affect mouse or pen input. You can edit a displayed saved revision directly; the first completed gesture starts a working copy from that revision.</p>
  <p>Select <gui>Crop</gui> in the toolbar or <gui>Edit</gui> menu, then drag the rectangle with the pen or mouse or enter exact pixel bounds. The editing tools remain available when the displayed drawing is writable and <gui>Show Markup</gui> is on; they are disabled when no drawing is displayed, it is not writable, or <gui>Show Markup</gui> is off. A pending crop on a saved revision is only a proposal: <gui>Cancel</gui> or <key>Escape</key> leaves the revision and all drafts unchanged. Only <gui>Apply Crop</gui> commits the crop to a working copy of the displayed revision. Switching to <gui>Pencil</gui> or <gui>Eraser</gui> discards the pending crop. <gui>Apply Crop</gui>, <gui>Cancel</gui>, and <key>Escape</key> return to the drawing tool selected before <gui>Crop</gui>. Crop does not resample pixels, and zoom changes only the view. No new shortcuts are assigned; Tab and arrow keys use native toolbar navigation, and Edit menu mnemonics remain available.</p>
  <p>Select the <gui>Zoom In</gui> (+) or <gui>Zoom Out</gui> (−) magnifier, then click or tap the drawing to zoom at that point. Press <key>Escape</key> to return to the previous editing tool without undoing zoom. A pending crop stays in place. You can also choose <gui>Zoom In</gui> or <gui>Zoom Out</gui> from the <gui>View</gui> menu for an immediate step around the center of the view.</p>
  <p>Hover the stylus over the drawing and press a side button to zoom at the pointer without switching tools. The side buttons reported as button 2 and button 3 zoom in and out, respectively. This does not change a pending crop and is unavailable during a stroke or crop drag.</p>
  <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Fit</gui></guiseq> to show the full drawing in the current viewport, or <guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Actual Size</gui></guiseq> for 100%. Fit works on the working copy and saved revisions, and it preserves any pending crop. It changes only the display scale, not the drawing or its attachments. For tiled originals, detail updates in the background as you zoom; the first reduced view can take longer while Canvas prepares its temporary cache. Zoom Out can return to Fit even when Fit is below 25%.</p>
</page>
